---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import ModesCalculator from '../../components/ModesCalculator.tsx';

const title = 'Room modes';
const description =
  'Room mode calculator for home cinemas: axial, tangential and oblique modes up to 300 Hz, mode density, pressure at the listening position and the effect of one or more subwoofers.';

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'What are room modes?',
    a: 'Low notes bounce back and forth between the walls and form “standing waves”. For some notes this makes one spot very loud and another almost silent – depending on where you sit. Almost every home cinema has this problem in the bass.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How are room modes calculated?',
    a: 'For a rectangular room, f = c/2 · √((nx/L)² + (ny/W)² + (nz/H)²), where c is the speed of sound (343 m/s at 20 °C), L, W, H are length, width and height, and nx, ny, nz are whole numbers starting at 0.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do multiple subwoofers help?',
    a: 'Yes, if they are in the right places. Two subwoofers on opposite walls even each other out for some notes. According to Todd Welti (Harman), one subwoofer in the middle of each wall gives the most even bass across all seats; two in the middle of opposite walls are almost as good. More than four isn’t worth it.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does this replace a measurement?',
    a: 'No. It shows which notes and which seats are likely to have problems. How strong they really are in your room, only a measurement can tell – for example with the free program REW and a measuring microphone.',
  },
];

const jsonLd = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'WebApplication',
    name: 'Plan My Cinema – Room modes',
    url: 'https://plan-my-cinema.com/en/room-modes/',
    applicationCategory: 'UtilitiesApplication',
    operatingSystem: 'Web',
    inLanguage: 'en',
    offers: { '@type': 'Offer', price: '0', priceCurrency: 'EUR' },
    description,
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'FAQPage',
    mainEntity: faq.map((f) => ({ '@type': 'Question', name: f.q, acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a } })),
  },
];
---
<Base lang="en" page="modes" title={title} description={description} jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <div class="page-head">
    <h1>Room <span>modes</span></h1>
    <p>Which bass notes boom in your room and which almost vanish – and what reaches your seat. Room size and seats come from your saved room.</p>
  </div>

  <ModesCalculator client:load lang="en" />

  <article class="prose">
    <h2>What is this about?</h2>
    <p>
      Low notes – the bass – bounce back and forth between the walls of your room. As they do, the sound waves keep meeting each other. Sometimes
      they add up, sometimes they cancel out. Think of waves in a bathtub: in some spots the water sloshes up high, in others it barely moves. These
      “standing waves” are called <strong>room modes</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The result: some bass notes boom far too loudly while others almost disappear. Which notes those are depends on the size of your room. The
      calculator shows you where it happens.
    </p>

    <h3>Why the bass sounds different in different places</h3>
    <p>
      Every room mode has loud spots and quiet spots. Along the walls and in the corners every mode is loud – that is why a subwoofer in a corner
      sounds so powerful: it sets all the modes going. Where you sit decides which notes you hear loudly and which quietly. In the middle of the room,
      for example, the very lowest note often goes missing.
    </p>

    <h3>What helps: more than one subwoofer</h3>
    <p>
      Two subwoofers on opposite walls can even each other out for some notes, so the room booms less. Todd Welti, a researcher at Harman, tested
      how many subwoofers you need and where they should go. His answer for a rectangular room: one subwoofer in the middle of each wall gives the
      most even bass across all seats. Two in the middle of opposite walls are almost as good. More than four isn’t worth it. You’ll find these
      layouts in the calculator under “Presets”.
    </p>

    <h3>SBA and DBA: whole walls of subwoofers</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Bass arrays</strong> go one step further. In an <strong>SBA</strong> (single bass array) several subwoofers are spread evenly across the
      front wall. Together they push the bass towards the back like one smooth wave, so the modes between the side walls, floor and ceiling hardly
      get going. A <strong>DBA</strong> (double bass array) adds the same layout on the back wall. Those rear subwoofers play the sound again – flipped
      upside down, at exactly the moment the wave from the front arrives. That way the wave is “swallowed” instead of bouncing back. Pick “SBA” or
      “DBA” at the top of the calculator.
    </p>

    <h3>Several seats</h3>
    <p>
      When several people are watching, each of them hears slightly different bass. Enter all seats under “Seating rows”. The calculator then shows,
      for every seat, which notes are loud or quiet there and how big the differences between the seats are. Each subwoofer preset shows a number:
      the smaller it is, the more alike the bass sounds on every seat.
    </p>

    <h3>How exact is this?</h3>
    <p>
      The calculator assumes an empty, rectangular room with solid walls. Furniture, rugs, doors and thin walls change the sound. So it shows you
      where problems are likely – how strong they really are, only a measurement with a measuring microphone can tell.
    </p>

    <details class="tech">
      <summary>For the technically minded: how we calculate</summary>


    <h3>Modes in a box</h3>
    <p>In a rectangular room with rigid walls, the modes sit at</p>
    <p><code>f = c/2 · √((nx/L)² + (ny/W)² + (nz/H)²)</code></p>
    <p>
      The speed of sound depends on air temperature: <code>c = 331.3 · √(1 + T/273.15)</code> m/s (dry-air approximation), which gives 343 m/s at
      20 °C. The calculator includes every mode up to 300 Hz.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Axial modes</strong> bounce between two opposite walls and are the strongest.</li>
      <li><strong>Tangential modes</strong> involve four surfaces and are weaker.</li>
      <li><strong>Oblique modes</strong> involve all six surfaces and are the weakest.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Sloped ceilings, L-shaped rooms or open doorways change the picture; for those rooms the result is only a rough guide.</p>

    <h3>What reaches the seat?</h3>
    <p>
      Every mode has pressure peaks (loud) and nulls (quiet). Walls and corners are always peaks, so a sub in a corner drives every mode. Your seat
      decides which ones you hear: sit in the middle of the room and the first length and width modes all but disappear, while the second length
      mode is at full strength.
    </p>
    <p>
      The “Modes at the listening position” chart multiplies the two: how hard the subs drive a mode and how much of it arrives at the seat.
      Damping from furniture, absorbers or flexible walls is not included. The split into null (below 0.3), transition and peak (above 0.7) is our
      own simplification.
    </p>

    <h3>Multiple subwoofers</h3>
    <p>
      Two subs on opposite walls are in opposite phase for the first mode along that axis, so it is barely driven – while the second mode adds up.
      Four subs at the wall midpoints or in the corners can calm several axes at once. Todd Welti (Harman) investigated this with
      simulations and measurements (“Subwoofers: Optimum Number and Locations”; companion AES paper “How Many Subwoofers are Enough?”, 2002). For a
      rectangular room with the seats in the centre or centre-rear, he found: one sub at each of the four wall midpoints evens out the seats best but
      gives less deep bass; two subs at opposite wall midpoints are very nearly as good with much better low-frequency output; four subs in the
      corners have good deep bass but even out the seats slightly less than the midpoints. He sees no justification for more than four. The presets in
      the calculator offer these layouts.
    </p>
    <p>
      The calculator assumes all subs play at the same level and in phase. With DSP (such as miniDSP) and a measurement you can set level, delay and
      EQ per sub – which often helps more than position alone.
    </p>

    <h3>SBA and DBA</h3>
    <p>
      Besides individual subs, the calculator plans two bass arrays. A <strong>single bass array (SBA)</strong> is an even grid of subs on the front
      wall: their contributions to the width and height modes cancel, so those modes are barely excited. Length modes remain, which is why an SBA needs a
      heavily damped back wall. A <strong>double bass array (DBA)</strong> adds the same grid on the back wall, fed the same signal inverted and delayed
      by the travel time over the room length, so that it cancels the wave arriving from the front instead of reflecting it.
    </p>
    <p>
      The calculator adds all subs with their polarity and delay, phase-correct at each mode frequency. Next to the seat-to-seat difference it shows the
      average mode strength at the seats – a DBA mainly improves that figure because it cancels the length modes at every seat alike. The model assumes
      rigid walls and an empty rectangular room, where a DBA works ideally. In real rooms furniture, doors, windows and flexible walls reduce the
      effect, so measuring to fine-tune the delay is worthwhile.
    </p>
    <p>
      Sources: grid formula (position = (2n + 1) · wall size ÷ (2 · count)), cut-off f = c ÷ (2 · sub spacing) and delay t = room length ÷ c from the
      Wikipedia article “Double bass array” (retrieved 2026-11-09); delay by the travel time over the room length, inverted polarity and identical
      front and rear layouts from D. Schmelzer, Nubert electronic, “Kompensation von Raummoden mittels einer doppelten Subwooferanordnung (DBA)”,
      31 Jan 2005. The method goes back to Celestinos and Nielsen (CABS, Journal of the Audio Engineering Society 56, 2008; full text not read).
    </p>
    <h3>Several seats</h3>
    <p>
      With several people watching, every seat hears different bass: a mode that peaks at the main seat may vanish in a null one row back. Enter the
      seats under “Seating rows” (the same ones the speaker planner uses). The calculator shows the spectrum for each seat and compares them: for every
      mode up to 120 Hz it reports the gap between the strongest and weakest seat. Each sub preset shows the average gap – the smaller, the more even the
      bass across all seats. The 120 Hz and 10 dB limits are our own choice; the model is simplified and no substitute for measuring.
    </p>
    <h3>Mode density</h3>
    <p>
      At low frequencies the modes stand alone and far apart – that is where the familiar boomy notes and holes come from. Higher up they crowd
      together and the room behaves more evenly. The “Mode density” chart shows where that happens in your room.
    </p>
    </details>

    <h2>FAQ</h2>
    {faq.map((f) => (
      <details>
        <summary>{f.q}</summary>
        <p>{f.a}</p>
      </details>
    ))}
  </article>
</Base>
